
=document :!auto-toc
=begin rakudoc
=TITLE An online editor for RakuDoc v2
=SUBTITLE
This tool can be accessed online or run locally (see the relevant document in the samples).
=head Preface

Several source documents can be pulled from the container - these are listed in the drop-down box on the far left.

The documents include this introduction, the specification and exposition files for RakuDoc v2, a render test file, a citation sample file, 
and some tutorial files.

It is also possible to upload your own sources to the browser, edit them then download either the edited RakuDoc source or the rendered HTML.

This Rakudoc editor is still under development, so new features will be added in due course.
The tool was tested using a recent version of the Firefox browser. 
Improvements are welcome via PRs at its L<Github repository|https://github.com/finanalyst/RakuDoc-Browser-Editor>.

The left-hand editor plane is an embedded instance of the L<ACE editor|https://ace.c9.io/>.
There is L<a list of shortcuts | https://github.com/ajaxorg/ace/wiki/Default-Keyboard-Shortcuts>
and C<F1> pops a searchable command list. Select C<Ctrl>B<|>C<Command>-B<,> to get the configuration menu (C<Esc> to exit).
This allows a user to select the theme and keyboard bindings, among others. To remove syntax highlighting, select the I<Text> mode.

=head User Interface

=numhead2 Getting source files

Several RakuDoc source fiies are included in the container running this editor. They can be chosen and modified at will.
Note that some are quite long and may take a time (several minutes) to render.

=numhead3 Select included file 

Click on B<Samples & tutorials>, which will pop up a drop down selection, choose the file you want.

=numhead3 Upload your own source

Click on B<Upload>, which will pop up a file chooser interface that is dependent on your browser 
and OS. It is set to look for only files with the C<.rakudoc> file extension.
=numhead2 Download a file

The rakudoc source or the rendered HTML can be downloaded. The precise process will depend on the browser and OS.

click on B<Download>, which will pop up a form with a suggested filename and a default C<.rakudoc> extension.
The file name can be changed. Selecting the C<.rakudoc> file format will download the RakuDoc source, while selecting the C<.html> file format
will download the rendered HTML.

=numhead2 Rendering and editing

By default, RakuDoc source is sent to the container for rendering half a second after changes have been made in the source. Changes are not sent if
the browser has not received the most recent rendering (see the State indicator). For long files or when writing long sections of source, 
immediate rendering can be distracting, so the rendering process can be switched to I<manual>.

=numhead3 Auto

Check the I<Render>B<Auto> setting by clicking on the B<Auto> button I<This is the default>.
Changes will be sent after a half second delay, unless rendering is proceeding.
=numhead3 Manual

Check the I<Render>B<Manual> setting by click on the B<Manual> button. Changes will not be sent.
=numhead3 Force a rendering

Clicking on the B<Force rendering> button to send changes will be send immediately, unless a previous 
rendering is being processed.

=numhead2 HTML framework 

The RakuDoc renderer has two (currently) HTML renderers: a minimal rendering in which all the CSS and JS code are included in the file, and 
one that requires an online connection to access the Bulma CSS framework and online plugins. 

=numhead3 Minimal - default

Check the I<Framework> B<Minimal> setting by clicking on the B<Minimal> button I<This is the default>.

=numhead3 Bulma

Check the I<Framework> B<Bulma & plugins> setting by click on the B<Bulma & plugins> button.
=numhead The State of the rendering process

The state is shown by an icon in the I<State> section. Hovering over the icon will give a description.

There are four states:
=for item :bullet<<\c[black circle]>>
I<Success> A rendering has been returned and was was successful in that an HTML file was created. Note, however, that 
errors in the RakuDoc 
may still exist, but these should be indicated in the warning section at the end of the rendering.
=for item :bullet<<\c[bullseye]>>
I<Failed> The RakuDoc source could not be parsed, and the parser message is returned.
=for item :bullet<<\c[heavy greek cross]>>
I<Changes> Changes have been made in the RakuDoc source that have not been rendered. This will occur if the Render 
process has been toggled 
to I<Manual> and the source file has been edited, or changes have been made while a previous set of changes is still being rendered.
=for item :bullet<<\c[black star]>>
I<Processing> The renderer is still rendering the file. This will be shown when the latest changes have been sent to the 
Renderer, but the rendered text has not been returned.

=end rakudoc